.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt M2 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm m2
.Nd Line-oriented macro processor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar name=value No ...
.Op Ar file No ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a line-oriented macro processor, a
.Dq little brother
to the
.Xr m4 1
macro processor found on Unix systems.
It is based on an earlier program, m1, written by Jon Bentley.
Like other macro processors,
the program can perform several functions, including:
.Bl -enum
.It
Define and expand macros.
Macros have two parts, a name and a body.
All occurrences of a macro's name are replaced with the macro's body.
Macro expansion may include parameters.
Short macros can be defined on the command line by using the form
.Ql NAME=VAL ,
or
.Ql NAME=
to define with empty value.
.It
Include files.  Special
.Ic @include
directives in a file are
replaced with the contents of the named file.
Includes can
be nested, with one included file including another.
Included files are processed for macros.
.It
Conditional text inclusion and exclusion.
Different parts of the text can be included in the final output, often
based upon whether a macro is or is not defined.
.It
Comments are removed from the output.
.Ic @comment
commands appear on a line of their own and are dropped from the output.
.Ic @rem No ... Ns Ic @
macros are embedded in the input text and produce no output.
Use
.Ic @ignore
for a multi-line comment.
.It
Manage multiple output data streams with diversions.
Different streams may be saved to different files.
.It
Partition symbol names into separate namespaces.
.Va Legal::approved
and
.Va Audit::approved
are both co-existing symbols named
.Dq approved .
.El
.Ss m2 Syntax
.Nm
does not scan
.Dq tokens
or replace unadorned text: macro
substitution must be explicitly requested by enclosing the macro
name in
.Ql @
characters.
Thus, any occurrence of
.Ql @name@
in the input is replaced in the output by the corresponding value.
To compensate for the burden of having to manually invoke
macro expansion, there is conversely no need to
.Dq quote
identifiers to protect against unwanted or inadvertent replacement,
which, depending upon the insistence of
.Em other
macro processors, may
present quite a challenge.
Substitutions can occur multiple times in a line.
.Pp
.Em Example :
.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
@define Condition under
You are clearly @Condition@worked.
\ \ \ => You are clearly underworked.
.Ed
.Pp
No white space is allowed between
.Ql @
and the name, so a lone at\&-sign does not trigger macro processing in any way.
Thus, a line like
.Dl 100 dollars @ 5% annual interest.
is completely benign.
.Pp
Using the same character to delimit both the beginning and end of macro
invocations was a conscious design decision by m1's author, but it
presents challenges when attempting to parse more advanced syntax, such
as invoking macros in a nested fashion.
It is difficult to distinguish, purely lexically:
.Dl @one + 2@\~\~AND ALSO\~\~@three + 4@
from
.Dl @substr Symbol\~\~@Pos1 Field1@\~\~Length@
.Pp
To alleviate scanning ambiguities, any characters enclosed in
at\&-sign braces
.Ql @{...}
will be recursively scanned and expanded.
The text between the braces is implicitly interpreted as if it
were surrounded by
.Ql @
characters, so
.Ql @{SYMBOL}
is correct; it means
.Ql @SYMBOL@ .
Thus, the example above is correctly written:
.\"  See file tests/SUBSTR/002/examp.m2
.Dl @substr Symbol @{Pos1 Field1} @{Length}@
.\"  This syntax is admittedly klunky, but with repeated exposure,
.\"  it soon becomes almost numbingly bearable.
.Qq @{Length}
is still required to use braces even though there are no parameters
because you must still expand the value of the symbol
.Va Length .
.Pp
.Em Example :
.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
@data_list[@{my_key}]@
.Ed
uses the value in
.Va my_key
to look up data from the list
.Va data_list .
The
.Ql @{...}
is required whenever you invoke macros inside other macros, and failure
to do so will result in obscure and hard\&-to-debug errors.
.Pp
.Bl -inset
.It Em Functions :
Specifying more than one word between
.Ql @
characters, as in
.Dl @func AAA BBB CCC@
is used as a crude form of function invocation.
Macros can expand positional parameters whose actual values will be
supplied when the macro is called.
The definition should refer to $1, $2, etc.
${1} also works, so ${1}1 is distinguishable from $11.
$0 refers to the name of the macro itself.
$# is replaced with the actual number of parameters provided.
$* expands to each given parameter, separated by a space character.
When a definition refers to a parameter which is not supplied, an empty
string will be used as a default value.
You may supply more parameters than needed; excess parameters are
simply ignored.
.Pp
.Em Example :
.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
@define greet Hello, $1!  m2 sends you $0ings.
@greet world@
\ \ \ => Hello, world!  m2 sends you greetings.
.Ed
.Pp
.Em Note :
Parameters passed using this syntax are parsed by splitting on
white space.
This means that in:
.Dl @foo \[dq]a b\[dq] c@
foo is given three arguments, not two: '"a', 'b"', and 'c'.
To support parameters with embedded spaces, an alternate syntax
is supported:
.Dl @foo{a b}{c}@
will provide two arguments: 'a b' and 'c'.  In order for this syntax to
be recognized, there must be no whitespace between the function name and
the first open brace; and no text at all between the final closing brace
and the trailing
.Ql @ .
.Pp
.Em Example :
.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
@define foo Arg1='$1' Arg2='$2' Arg3='$3'  narg=$#
@foo "a b" c@
\ \ \ => Arg1='"a' Arg2='b"' Arg3='c'  narg=3
@foo{a b}{c}@
\ \ \ => Arg1='a b' Arg2='c' Arg3=''  narg=2
@foo{a b}{c}{}@
\ \ \ => Arg1='a b' Arg2='c' Arg3=''  narg=3
.Ed
.Pp
See the
.Sx Functions
section for a complete list of built-in functions.
.It Em Commands :
Control commands
.Po
such as
.Ic @if ,
.Ic @define ,
etc
.Pc
are distinguished by an
.Ql @
as the first character at the beginning of a line.
They consume the entire line.
Some commands have a
.Dq silent
alternate form, distinguished by a leading
.Ql s
in the command name, which prints fewer error messages or performs some
slightly altered function.
For example, the silent variant to include a file is
.Ic @sinclude .
See the
.Sx Commands
section for a complete list of built-in commands.
.It Em Namespaces :
.Nm
implements
.Dq namespaces
in a manner similar to, but not dependent upon, gawk's example.
A
.Dq qualified name
is an identifier that
consists of a namespace name, the qualification separator
.Qq :: ,
and then the symbol name proper.
A qualified name may reference an array or list and thus
may be followed with a bracketed key value to evaluate.
There is always a
.Dq current
namespace into which unadorned symbols (those not found elsewhere in
the search list) are defined.
The current namespace can be changed with the
.Ic @namespace
command; it can be retrieved with the
.Ic @ns@
function.
See section
.Sx Namespaces
for further details.
.It Em Lists and Arrays :
Lists are ordered collections with integer keys.
Arrays are unordered collections with arbitrary text keys.
Elements of both arrays and lists are accessed
by appending
.Bq Ar key
to the array or list name.
Lists and arrays must be declared before use.
See section
.Sx Lists and Arrays
for further details.
.It Em Line Continuation :
There may be occasions when you wish to continue a logical line
onto the next physical line.
It is common in many Unix programs for a
.Ql \e
character at the end of a line to do this.
However,
.Nm
is not in the business of altering, without being requested, text which
may be sent to other programs.
In order to continue lines, the characters
.Ic @\e
may be used at the end of a logical line, indicating that the next line shall
be joined to the previous without any intervening newline.
This results in
.Dq Va line1 Ns Va line2 .
This allows
.Nm
to work on files with backslashes without disturbing
them, while still providing a mechanism for line continuation that will
not be triggered spuriously.
If you wish to append the next line with a newline separator,
end the current line with
.Ic @\en .
This results in
.Dq Va line1 Ns Ic \en Ns Va line2 .
A third variant,
.Ic @\e- ,
removes leading whitespace from the subsequent line.
.Pp
.Em Example :
.Bd -literal -offset indent -compact
Foo@\e
  bar
\ \ \ => Foo  bar
Foo@\en
bar
\ \ \ => Foo
\ \ \    bar
Foo@\e-
  bar
\ \ \ => Foobar
.Ed
.El
.Ss Boolean Expressions
Boolean expressions are used in the
.Ic @if
and
.Ic @while
commands and the
.Ic @ifx@
function.
They support the
.Qq && ,
.Qq || ,
and
.Qq \&!
operators for logical AND, OR, and NOT, respectively.
The && and || operators will
.Dq short-circuit
and not evaluate their right-hand side terms if the result is
determined by the left-hand side.
Parentheses may be used to enforce evaluation order.
.Pp
The following boolean predicate functions return either True or False.
Arguments to these functions are scanned for macro expansion.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width elem_IN_array____ -offset indent
.It Fn canrun PROGRAM
True if
.Va PROGRAM
can be executed; checked with
.Xr sh 1
.Qq "command -v" .
See also
.Ic @execpath@ .
.It Fn defined SYMBOL
True if
.Va SYMBOL
is defined.
If the qualified namespace
.Qq ENV
is used, return True if the symbol is defined as a variable
in the process environment.
See also
.Ic @ifdef@ .
.It Fn exists FILE
True if
.Va FILE
exists.
.It Ar ELEM Cm in Ar ARR
True if
.Va ARR
(array or list) contains
.Va ELEM
and False if not.
.El
.Pp
Any other expression is evaluated as a mathematical expression and
considered True if the result is non-zero.
.Ss Commands
The following table lists control commands to
evaluate, control, or define macros for subsequent processing:
.Pp
.\" This table could use a little formatting TLC...
.Bl -tag -width @if(_not)_defined -offset indent
.It Ic @append Ar NAME TEXT
Add
.Va TEXT
to an already defined
.Va NAME .
If
.Va NAME
is a symbol,
.Va TEXT
is textually appended to its definition.
If
.Va NAME
is a list,
.Va TEXT
is added as the next element.
.It Ic @array Ar ARR
Declare
.Va ARR
to be an array.
The name must not be qualified.
See also
.Ic @list .
.It Ic @break
Interrupt execution of enclosing loop.
.Ic @break
is only valid inside a
.Ic @for ,
.Ic @foreach ,
or
.Ic @while
block.
.It Ic @case Ar NAME Oo Ar PREAMBLE Ns No ... Oc Oo Ic @of Va TEXT No ... Oc Oo Ic @otherwise No ... Oc Ic @endcase
Evaluate value of
.Va NAME ,
comparing it with
each successive
.Ic @of Va TEXT .
If a matching
.Ic @of
branch is found, execute the
.Va PREAMBLE
(if any) and the
of\&-branch text and commands.
If no
.Ic @of
text matches, use text from
.Ic @otherwise
block if present.
Execution of the
.Ic @otherwise
block does
.Sy not
execute the
.Va PREAMBLE .
.Po
The purpose of the preamble is to provide "boiler-plate" header text for
known cases.
The text and commands of the
.Ic @otherwise
block are expected
to compensate for the lack of a preamble.
.Pc
.It Ic @cleardivert Op Ar N No ...
Clear stream(s) specified by
.Va N .
If no arguments are provided then all streams are cleared.
See the
.Sx Streams and Diversions
section.
.It Ic @comment Op Ar TEXT
Comment; ignore line.  Also
.Ic @@ ,
.Ic @c ,
.Ic @; ,
and
.Ic @# .
.Ic @comment ,
.Ic @c ,
and
.Ic @@
must be followed by whitespace in order to be recognized as comment commands.
.Ic @;
and
.Ic @#
are recognized as comments whenever they appear in columns 1 and 2.
.It Ic @continue
Continue next iteration of enclosing loop.
.Ic @continue
is only valid inside a
.Ic @for ,
.Ic @foreach ,
or
.Ic @while
block.
.It Ic @data Ar LIS No ... Ic @enddata
Store all lines until
.Ic @enddata
into list
.Va LIS .
Any previously existing list entries are deleted.
.Va LIS
must declared with
.Ic @list .
See also
.Ic @filedata .
.It Ic @debug Op Ar TEXT
If debugging, send
.Va TEXT
to the debug file.
See also
.Va __DEBUG__ .
.It Ic @decr Ar NAME Op Ar N
Subtract
.Va N
(default 1) from an already defined
.Va NAME .
See note at
.Ic @incr .
.It Ic @default Ar NAME VAL
Like
.Ic @define ,
but no\&-op if
.Va NAME
already defined.
.It Ic @define Ar NAME Op Ar TEXT
Set value of
.Va NAME
to
.Va TEXT .
If
.Va TEXT
is missing, a value of 1 is assumed.
See also
.Ic @set .
.It Ic @divert Op Ar N
Divert subsequent text to stream
.Va N .
Default 0 resets to standard output.
If
.Va N
is negative, text is discarded.
No error message or action if argument is not an integer.
See the
.Sx Streams and Diversions
section.
.It Ic @divpop
Remove the current diversion and restore the previous diversion.
See also
.Ic @divpush .
.It Ic @divpush Ar N
Create a new diversion to stream
.Ar N
while saving the current one to be restored later.
See also
.Ic @divpop .
.It Ic @dump Op Ar WHAT Op Ar FILE
Output names and definitions to
.Va FILE .
.Va WHAT
can be one of
.Cm symbols ,
.Cm sequences ,
.Cm commands ,
.Cm names ,
or an integer, which is assumed to be a block number.
.\"   "@dump blocks" also works (poorly) and is deliberately undocumented.
If
.Va WHAT
is omitted,
.Cm symbols
is assumed.
If
.Va FILE
is omitted, output is sent to
.Va __DEBUGFILE__ .
.It Ic @dumpall Op Ar WHAT Op Ar FILE
Like
.Ic @dump
but include System symbols.
.It Ic @dumpdef Op Ar SYM No ...
Output definition of
.Va SYM
to
.Va __DEBUGFILE__ .
More than one symbol may be listed.
If no symbols are specified, all symbols are dumped.
.It Ic @echo Op Ar TEXT
Send
.Va TEXT
to standard error.
Also called
.Ic @errprint .
.Ic @warn
is similar, but adds the current file name and line number to output.
Variant
.Ic @secho
does not automatically add a newline to output.
.It Ic @else
Switch to the alternate condition branch of an
.Ic @if
block.
Only one
.Ic @else
statement is allowed per
.Ic @if
block.
.It Ic @endcase
Indicate the end of a
.Ic @case
structure.
Also called
.Ic @esac
thanks to Algol\ 68.
.It Ic @endcmd
Indicate the end of a
.Ic @newcmd
definition.
.It Ic @enddata
Indicate the end of a
.Ic @data
block.
Also called
.Ic @eod .
.It Ic @endif
Indicate the end of an
.Ic @if
or
.Ic @unless
condition block.
Also called
.Ic @fi ,
keeping the dream alive.
.It Ic @endlongdef
Indicate the end of a multi-line
.Ic @longdef .
.It Ic @endwhile
Indicate the end of a
.Ic @while
or
.Ic @until
loop.
Also called
.Ic @wend ,
by few.
.It Ic @error Op Ar TEXT
Send
.Va TEXT
to standard error; exit code 2.
Any diverted streams are discarded, although any wrapped text is evaluated.
For complete control over error messages, variant
.Ic @serror
prints only
.Va TEXT ,
omitting file and line number information.
.It Ic @esyscmd Ar CMDLINE Ns No ...
Execute
.Va CMDLINE
in a subshell.
Standard output is captured into the output stream as normal.
Standard input is set to
.Pa /dev/null ,
but standard error is not modified.
The command's exit code is saved in
.Va __SYSVAL__ .
.It Ic @eval Ar TEXT
Parse and evaluate
.Va TEXT
as a command.
.It Ic @exit Op Ar N
Immediately stop parsing; exit with status
.Va N .
If
.Va N
is omitted, the current exit status is unchanged.
Invalid
.Va N ,
values less than zero, or greater than 126, are changed to 1.
If variant
.Ic @sexit
is used, or if exit status is non-zero, diverted streams are discarded.
Commands deferred with
.Ic @wrap
are processed regardless of
.Va N
value.
See also
.Va __EXIT__ .
.It Ic @filedata Ar LIS FILE
Read each line from
.Va FILE
into list
.Va LIS .
Any previously existing list entries are deleted before reading file contents.
Variant
.Ic @sfiledata
does not print an error message if
.Va FILE
does not exist.
.Va LIS
must be declared as a list, therefore user code must say
.Ql @list LIS
before
.Ic @filedata
can be used.
See also
.Ic @data .
.It Ic @filedefine Ar NAME FILE
Read
.Va FILE
contents to define
.Va NAME .
This is not intended to be a full\&-blown file inputter (use
.Ic @filedata
for that) but rather just to read short snippets like a file path
or username.
Multi-line values are accepted but the final
trailing newline, if any, is stripped.
Variant
.Ic @sfiledefine
does not print an error message if
.Va FILE
does not exist.
See also
.Ic @define .
.It Ic @for Ar VAR START END Oo Ar INCR Oc No ... Ic @next Ar VAR
Execute a loop with
.Va VAR
taking on successive
integer values specified by
.Va START
and
.Va END .
Default
.Va INCR
is 1.
.\" .It Ic @foreach Ar VAR ARR No | Ar LIS ... Ic @next Ar VAR
.It Ic @foreach Ar VAR NAME No ... Ic @next Ar VAR
Execute a loop with
.Va VAR
taking on successive values of
.Em keys
of
.Va NAME ,
which must be an array or list.
.Em Note :
Different Awk implementations may return array keys in differing order.
However, lists and data created with
.Ic @data
or
.Ic @filedata
are guaranteed to return their integer indices in numerical order.
.It Ic @if Ar EXPR No ... Oo Ic @else No ... Oc Ic @endif
Include subsequent lines of text if boolean expression is True.
If
.Va EXPR
is a simple NAME, check if NAME is True.
See section
.Sx Boolean Expressions .
.It Ic @ignore Ar PATTERN
Discard input until a line that matches regular expression
.Va PATTERN
is found.
.It Ic @import Ar LIB FILE
Like
.Ic @include
but the library name
.Va LIB
is added to the namespace search list.
See also
.Va __NSPATH__ .
.It Ic @include Ar FILE
Read and process contents of
.Va FILE .
The current namespace is temporarily set to
.Qq m2
while
.Va FILE
is being processed.
Variant
.Ic @sinclude
does not print an error message if
.Va FILE
does not exist.
See also
.Ic @nsinclude
and
.Ic @paste .
.It Ic @incr Ar NAME Op Ar N
Add
.Va N
(default 1) to an already defined
.Va NAME .
.Ic @incr
and
.Ic @decr
may be used on both symbols and sequences.
.Em Note :
Unlike
.Xr m4 1 ,
.Ic @incr
modifies the named symbol and produces no expansion output.
.It Ic @initialize Ar NAME VAL
Like
.Ic @define ,
but error if
.Va NAME
already defined.
.It Ic @input Op Ar NAME
Read a single line from keyboard to define
.Va NAME .
No prompt is issued; if you want one, use
.Ic @echo .
If
.Va NAME
is omitted, value is stored in symbol
.Va __INPUT__ .
.It Ic @list Ar LIS
Declare
.Va LIS
to be a list.
The name must not be qualified.
See also
.Ic @array .
.It Ic @literal Ar PATTERN
Read and print input literally, without interpretation, until line that
matches regular expression
.Va PATTERN .
.It Ic @local Ar NAME
Declare
.Va NAME
as a symbol local to the current level.
The name must not be qualified.
.It Ic @longdef Ar NAME No ... Ic @endlongdef
Set value of
.Va NAME
to all lines until
.Ic @endlongdef .
.It Ic @namespace Ar NAMESPACE
Make
.Va NAMESPACE
the current namespace.
The name should not be quoted.
See also
.Ic @ns@ .
.It Ic @newcmd Ar NAME No ... Ic @endcmd
Create a user command
.Va NAME
whose definition will be all lines until
.Ic @endcmd .
.It Ic @next Ar VAR
Continue execution of next iteration of enclosing
.Ic @for
or
.Ic @foreach
loop.
.It Ic @nextfile
Ignore remainder of current file and resume processing with the next file.
.It Ic @nsinclude Ar FILE
Similar to
.Ic @include
but the current namespace remains unchanged.
.It Ic @of Ar TEXT
Begin a new branch of an enclosing
.Ic @case
structure matching
.Va TEXT
at run time.
Every branch's
.Va TEXT
label must be unique.
.It Ic @otherwise
Begin a new branch of an enclosing
.Ic @case
structure which is executed if no
.Ic @of
branch triggers.
Only one
.Ic @otherwise
branch is permitted per
.Ic @case
structure.
.It Ic @paste Ar FILE
Insert
.Va FILE
contents literally, with no changes or macros.
Variant
.Ic @spaste
does not print an error message if
.Va FILE
does not exist.
.It Ic @readonly Ar NAME
Make
.Va NAME
unchangeable \(em cannot be undone.
.It Ic @return Op Ar VALUE
Cease execution of a user command.
.Ic @return
is only valid inside a
.Ic @newcmd
definition.
.It Ic @sequence Ar ID Cm ACTION Op Ar arg
Create and manage sequences.
See the
.Sx Sequences
section.
.It Ic @set Ar NAME Op Ar TEXT
Like
.Ic @define ,
but if
.Va TEXT
is missing,
.Va NAME
is set to the empy string.
See also
.Ic @define .
.It Ic @shell Ar PATTERN Op Ar PROG
Evaluate input until
.Va PATTERN
and send raw data to
.Va PROG .
Output stream is captured; shell exit status is stored in
.Va __SYSVAL__ .
If
.Va PROG
is not specified, the value of symbol
.Va M2_SHELL
will be used if defined; if not, the value of environment variable
.Ev SHELL
will be used.
If also not defined, the value of
.Va __PROG__[sh]
will be used.
.Va PROG
should default to a reasonable shell, but might be overridden to
run other useful programs such as an alternative shell, an email message
reader, or
.Pa /usr/bin/bc .
It must be a program that functions as a filter (in the Unix sense,
i.e., reading from standard input and writing to standard output).
Standard error is not redirected, so any errors will appear on the terminal.
.It Ic @split Ar NAME LIS Op Ar FS
Split value of
.Va NAME
into N individual fields, storing them in
.Va LIS
elements 1..N.
Any previous entries in
.Va LIS
are deleted.
If
.Va FS
is specified, use it as field separator.
If not and symbol
.Va __FS__
is defined, use its value as field separator.
If neither condition is true, use Awk's default
.Va FS ,
which means fields will be split on whitespace.
See also
.Ic @join@ ,
.Ic @list .
.It Ic @syscmd Ar CMDLINE Ns No ...
Execute
.Va CMDLINE
using Awk's
.Fn system
function.
Standard output and standard error are both discarded.
The command's exit code is saved in
.Va __SYSVAL__ .
.It Ic @tracemode Op Ar FLAG Ns No ...
With no arguments, reset trace flags to default value.
Preceding flag with
.Ql +
or
.Ql \-
will add to or remove from trace flags;
otherwise, overwrite trace flags with new value.
See also symbol
.Va __TRACEMODE__
and section
.Sx Tracing .
